  "prompt_text": "The figure is a schematic of a developing brain region, contrasting the subpallium on the left with the pallium on the right and highlighting how neurons are generated and migrate between them. Purple elongated cells, labeled as cortical interneurons, appear to originate in the subpallium and move tangentially into the pallium. In the pallium, yellow round cells marked as intermediate progenitor cells sit around the ventricular zone and subventricular zone near the ventricle, suggesting a distinct local progenitor population. Some yellow cells are drawn with mitotic spindle-like structures, indicating division.\n\nThe overall message is that the study combines several experimental and computational approaches to understand these cell populations and their movements. Arrows point from the tissue schematic to methods such as single-cell tracing, single-cell transcriptomics, histology with tissue clearing, microfluidic migration assays, time-lapse analysis, and in silico modelling/data acquisition. Together, the image conveys an integrated framework for linking cell identity, lineage, proliferation, and migratory behavior during forebrain development. Visually, the key comparison is between migratory interneurons entering the pallium and proliferative intermediate progenitors located around the ventricular surface.",
